+# Decision: Transport for delivering committed revisions to browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision; connected browsers must reflect those revisions within the 15-second freshness goal, recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes offline, and hold up at 800 simultaneous browsers with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second.
+
+How committed revisions travel from the server to browsers is the choice that sizes three downstream nodes at once — the change store (what revision history and publication path it must keep), delivery (what it manages between server and browser), and browser sync (how a browser subscribes, recovers, and catches up). Choosing the transport wrong is expensive to reverse because all three are built around its shape. The freshness goal is reachable by more than one transport, so the choice turns on cost, operational ownership, and how tightly the board is tied to outside infrastructure rather than on raw capability.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option: all three transports can meet the 15-second freshness goal, the 20-minute recovery requirement, and the throughput targets. Selection turns entirely on the weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor independence (major).** Whether the board can deliver updates without depending on an outside realtime provider — its credentials, its availability, its pricing, and its subscription model. A transport that couples delivery to a provider makes that provider a standing dependency of the core experience.
+- **Operational burden (major).** The connection, recovery, and state machinery the project must build and operate itself — connection capacity, reconnect handling, replayable revisions, and a reliable publication path.
+- **Recurring operating cost (moderate).** Ongoing per-period spend the transport imposes, separate from the infrastructure the project already runs.
+- **Update latency (moderate).** How quickly a committed revision reaches a connected browser. Faster than the 15-second goal is useful but not required; sub-second is not required.
+- **Connection fan-out at scale (moderate).** How the transport sustains 800 concurrent browsers and opening-time bursts — whether that capacity is the project's to provision or absorbed elsewhere.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board on a fixed ~5-second cadence over ordinary HTTPS. Delivery stays request/response with no persistent connection; the change store answers cursor queries ("everything after revision N"); browsers run a polling loop and advance their cursor. Recovery is just a wider cursor query after a gap.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — ordinary HTTPS, no outside provider.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless request/response, no connection sessions to manage, no reconnect logic; the store needs only cursor queries.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no per-period vendor fee; cost is request load on infrastructure already run.
+- Update latency: **weak** — up to the poll interval plus request time, a few seconds of lag; within the 15-second goal but the slowest of the three and short of the fast updates wanted.
+- Connection fan-out at scale: **partial** — 800 browsers polling every five seconds is a sustained, repeated read load, though stateless and straightforward to scale horizontally.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest, cheapest operation, bought with repeated reads and seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The project runs its own WebSocket endpoint. Browsers hold a persistent connection, subscribe to their event, and receive committed revisions pushed as they commit. The change store keeps a replayable revision history and a reliable publication path (an outbox) that hands each committed revision to delivery in order; delivery manages the socket sessions and publishes to subscribers; browser sync detects dropped connections, reconnects, and catches up from its last acknowledged revision.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — no realtime provider; delivery runs on infrastructure the project already operates.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity, reconnect and catch-up handling, replayable revisions, and reliable publication.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no per-period vendor fee.
+- Update latency: **strong** — revisions are pushed as they commit; sub-second is achievable, well inside the goal.
+- Connection fan-out at scale: **partial** — the project must provision capacity for 800 concurrent connections and opening-time bursts itself.
+
+**Summary.** Fast, provider-free updates bought with connection and reconnect operations the project owns.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider carries the fan-out. The server publishes committed revisions to the provider; browsers subscribe through the provider's client SDK and subscription model. The change store reconciles its revisions against what the provider has published; delivery wraps the provider and holds its credentials; browser sync uses the provider's subscription and recovery primitives.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — delivery depends on the provider's availability, pricing, credentials, and subscription model.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider manages connections, scale, and much of recovery; the project wraps it and reconciles publication.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — recurring per-period fees that scale with connections and messages.
+- Update latency: **strong** — purpose-built low-latency push.
+- Connection fan-out at scale: **strong** — the provider absorbs concurrent connections and bursts.
+
+**Summary.** The quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations, bought with recurring fees and a standing provider dependency.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It is strong on both major-importance criteria the choice turns on — vendor independence and (with polling) recurring operating cost — while delivering the fast updates polling cannot. Its weakness is operational burden, the second major criterion: the project takes on connection capacity, reconnect and catch-up handling, replayable revisions, and reliable publication, and must provision its own fan-out for 800 browsers and opening-time bursts. That burden is accepted deliberately as the price of keeping the board free of a realtime vendor.
+
+Polling is eliminated not by any gate but by update latency: it is the operationally simplest and cheapest option, but a few seconds of poll lag is the opposite of the fast updates wanted, and nothing about owning the transport is hard enough to justify giving that up. The hosted service matches self-hosted on latency and beats it on operational burden and fan-out, but loses the major vendor-independence criterion outright and adds recurring fees — it trades exactly the independence the project most wants to keep for operational relief the project is willing to do without.
+
+The decision is consistent with the standing tenet that self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted; it does not turn on that tenet, which is neutral between these options. It turns instead on valuing vendor independence above minimum operational burden — a lean this decision makes locally and no current tenet states.
+
+**Downstream obligations.**
+- **Change store** must keep a replayable event revision history and a reliable publication path (outbox) that hands each committed revision to delivery in order.
+- **Delivery** must manage WebSocket sessions, publish committed revisions to subscribed browsers, and own connection capacity for 800 concurrent browsers plus opening-time bursts.
+- **Browser sync** must subscribe to its event, detect dropped connections, reconnect, and catch up from its last acknowledged revision.
+
+**Forecloses.** No managed realtime provider in the pilot; the board must operate its own connection capacity and reconnection handling.
